1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,776
Here is a block of two hundred and fifty-six
addresses. We

2
00:00:04,776 --> 00:00:09,552
want four equal networks, with every address
accounted for exactly once.

3
00:00:09,552 --> 00:00:14,329
Where should the boundaries go? Guessing
decimal numbers makes this feel

4
00:00:14,329 --> 00:00:19,105
like a memorization exercise. But the
boundaries come from a much

5
00:00:19,105 --> 00:00:23,881
simpler picture: some address bits stay fixed,
and the others are

6
00:00:23,881 --> 00:00:28,657
free to change. We are going to move that
dividing line

7
00:00:28,657 --> 00:00:33,433
and watch four networks appear.

8
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:38,148
An IPv4 address contains thirty-two bits. We
normally display them as

9
00:00:38,148 --> 00:00:42,862
four decimal numbers separated by dots, with
eight bits represented by

10
00:00:42,862 --> 00:00:47,576
each number. Those eight-bit groups are called
octets. Expand the final

11
00:00:47,576 --> 00:00:52,290
octet and the familiar address becomes a row
of switches, each

12
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:57,005
either zero or one. The dotted format is
useful for reading.

13
00:00:57,005 --> 00:01:01,719
The bit view is useful for seeing which
combinations belong to

14
00:01:01,719 --> 00:01:06,433
the same address block.

15
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:11,148
In our example, the block is 192.168.1.0 slash
twenty-four. Slash twenty-four

16
00:01:11,148 --> 00:01:15,862
says that the first twenty-four bit positions
are fixed. Every address

17
00:01:15,862 --> 00:01:20,576
in the block shares that beginning. The final
eight positions can

18
00:01:20,576 --> 00:01:25,290
change, giving us different addresses within
the block. This boundary is

19
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:30,005
what the prefix describes. It does not mean
the address contains

20
00:01:30,005 --> 00:01:34,719
a slash bit; the slash is our way of stating
how

21
00:01:34,719 --> 00:01:39,433
many positions are shared.

22
00:01:39,433 --> 00:01:44,210
Each free bit has two choices. Two free bits
make four

23
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:48,986
combinations; three make eight. Keep doubling
until eight free bits give

24
00:01:48,986 --> 00:01:53,762
two to the power of eight, or two hundred and
fifty-six

25
00:01:53,762 --> 00:01:58,538
combinations. In the final octet, they run
from zero through two

26
00:01:58,538 --> 00:02:03,314
hundred and fifty-five. We have counted the
entire block, including addresses

27
00:02:03,314 --> 00:02:08,090
with special roles. We will assign those roles
after dividing the

28
00:02:08,090 --> 00:02:12,867
block into its smaller networks.

29
00:02:12,867 --> 00:02:17,390
Move the boundary one position right. The
first formerly free bit

30
00:02:17,390 --> 00:02:21,914
now identifies which half we mean: zero on one
side, one

31
00:02:21,914 --> 00:02:26,438
on the other. Each half has seven free bits
left, so

32
00:02:26,438 --> 00:02:30,962
each contains one hundred and twenty-eight
addresses. The total has not

33
00:02:30,962 --> 00:02:35,486
changed. We have simply described the original
space as two smaller

34
00:02:35,486 --> 00:02:40,010
blocks. A longer fixed prefix leaves fewer
possibilities inside each individual

35
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:44,533
block.

36
00:02:44,533 --> 00:02:49,248
Move the boundary once more. Each half splits
in two, giving

37
00:02:49,248 --> 00:02:53,962
four groups identified by zero-zero, zero-one,
one-zero, and one-one. Two additional

38
00:02:53,962 --> 00:02:58,676
fixed bits create two to the power of two
subnets. Starting

39
00:02:58,676 --> 00:03:03,390
at slash twenty-four and adding two gives
slash twenty-six. The prefix

40
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:08,105
length, the number of groups, and the visible
split are three

41
00:03:08,105 --> 00:03:12,819
views of the same operation. No address has
been created or

42
00:03:12,819 --> 00:03:17,533
lost along the way.

43
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:22,119
Each new subnet has six free host bits. Two to
the

44
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:26,705
power of six gives sixty-four addresses per
subnet. Check the accounting:

45
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:31,290
four subnets multiplied by sixty-four
addresses equals our original two hundred

46
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:35,876
and fifty-six. This is a useful way to catch a
wrong

47
00:03:35,876 --> 00:03:40,462
boundary. If the groups overlap, leave gaps,
or contain the wrong

48
00:03:40,462 --> 00:03:45,048
number of combinations, their totals will stop
matching the space we

49
00:03:45,048 --> 00:03:49,633
started with.

50
00:03:49,633 --> 00:03:54,471
Keep the six host bits at zero and change only
the

51
00:03:54,471 --> 00:03:59,310
two subnet bits. Zero-zero begins at zero.
Zero-one begins at sixty-four.

52
00:03:59,310 --> 00:04:04,148
One-zero begins at one hundred and twenty-
eight. One-one begins at one

53
00:04:04,148 --> 00:04:08,986
hundred and ninety-two. Those are the four
network starts in the

54
00:04:08,986 --> 00:04:13,824
final octet. Their spacing is sixty-four
because each block contains sixty-four

55
00:04:13,824 --> 00:04:18,662
combinations. The decimal pattern comes
directly from the binary positions, rather

56
00:04:18,662 --> 00:04:23,500
than a separate shortcut to memorize.

57
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:28,086
A subnet mask marks fixed prefix positions
with ones and the

58
00:04:28,086 --> 00:04:32,671
remaining positions with zeros. For slash
twenty-six, the final octet begins

59
00:04:32,671 --> 00:04:37,257
one-one, followed by six zeros. The first two
positions have values

60
00:04:37,257 --> 00:04:41,843
one hundred and twenty-eight and sixty-four.
Add them and the mask's

61
00:04:41,843 --> 00:04:46,429
last octet is one hundred and ninety-two. The
complete mask is

62
00:04:46,429 --> 00:04:51,014
255.255.255.192. Now the mask and our moving
boundary describe exactly the

63
00:04:51,014 --> 00:04:55,600
same division.

64
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:00,656
Take the address 192.168.1.70. The final
octet, seventy, is 01000110 in

65
00:05:00,656 --> 00:05:05,711
binary. Apply the mask 11000000 using bitwise
AND. A mask one

66
00:05:05,711 --> 00:05:10,767
keeps the corresponding address bit; a zero
clears it. The result

67
00:05:10,767 --> 00:05:15,822
is 01000000, or sixty-four. So this address
belongs to 192.168.1.64 slash

68
00:05:15,822 --> 00:05:20,878
twenty-six. We did not search four separate
lists. We removed the

69
00:05:20,878 --> 00:05:25,933
varying host part and exposed the subnet's
shared beginning.

70
00:05:25,933 --> 00:05:30,962
On an ordinary LAN subnet, the address with
all host bits

71
00:05:30,962 --> 00:05:35,990
zero identifies the network, and the address
with all host bits

72
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:41,019
one is its broadcast address. In a sixty-four-
address block, those two

73
00:05:41,019 --> 00:05:46,048
roles leave sixty-two addresses for hosts.
This is why total addresses

74
00:05:46,048 --> 00:05:51,076
and usable host addresses are different
counts. We are applying the

75
00:05:51,076 --> 00:05:56,105
ordinary LAN convention in this example,
rather than claiming that subtracting

76
00:05:56,105 --> 00:06:01,133
two is a universal rule for every possible
prefix.

77
00:06:01,133 --> 00:06:05,719
The first block spans zero through sixty-
three. Its network is zero,

78
00:06:05,719 --> 00:06:10,305
its broadcast is sixty-three, and hosts use
one through sixty-two. The

79
00:06:10,305 --> 00:06:14,890
second spans sixty-four through one hundred
and twenty-seven. Its hosts use

80
00:06:14,890 --> 00:06:19,476
sixty-five through one hundred and twenty-six.
Notice that this broadcast ends

81
00:06:19,476 --> 00:06:24,062
in one hundred and twenty-seven, not two
hundred and fifty-five. Broadcast

82
00:06:24,062 --> 00:06:28,648
follows the host-bit pattern inside the chosen
subnet, not a particular

83
00:06:28,648 --> 00:06:33,233
decimal ending.

84
00:06:33,233 --> 00:06:38,138
The third block spans one hundred and twenty-
eight through one hundred

85
00:06:38,138 --> 00:06:43,043
and ninety-one, with hosts between one hundred
and twenty-nine and one

86
00:06:43,043 --> 00:06:47,948
hundred and ninety. The fourth spans one
hundred and ninety-two through

87
00:06:47,948 --> 00:06:52,852
two hundred and fifty-five, with hosts from
one hundred and ninety-three

88
00:06:52,852 --> 00:06:57,757
through two hundred and fifty-four. Watch
their edges meet. Each block

89
00:06:57,757 --> 00:07:02,662
starts immediately after the previous block
ends, covering the original parent

90
00:07:02,662 --> 00:07:07,567
without an overlap or a missing address.

91
00:07:07,567 --> 00:07:12,214
Suppose a network needs fifty host addresses.
Five free bits give

92
00:07:12,214 --> 00:07:16,862
thirty-two total addresses, or thirty ordinary
LAN host addresses, which is

93
00:07:16,862 --> 00:07:21,510
too small. Six free bits give sixty-four total
and sixty-two usable,

94
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:26,157
which fits. Thirty-two minus six leaves a
slash-twenty-six prefix. Instead of

95
00:07:26,157 --> 00:07:30,805
starting with a memorized mask, we started
with the required capacity

96
00:07:30,805 --> 00:07:35,452
and counted the combinations needed to
accommodate it. The mask follows

97
00:07:35,452 --> 00:07:40,100
from that decision.

98
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:44,748
Compare last-octet values seventy and one
hundred. Masking each produces sixty-four,

99
00:07:44,748 --> 00:07:49,395
so they share this subnet. Now compare seventy
with one hundred

100
00:07:49,395 --> 00:07:54,043
and thirty. The latter produces one hundred
and twenty-eight, placing it

101
00:07:54,043 --> 00:07:58,690
in another subnet. Similar-looking decimal
addresses are not the deciding evidence.

102
00:07:58,690 --> 00:08:03,338
Their fixed prefix bits are. When a result
feels suspicious, return

103
00:08:03,338 --> 00:08:07,986
to the boundary and ask which bits were
allowed to vary

104
00:08:07,986 --> 00:08:12,633
inside the block.

105
00:08:12,633 --> 00:08:17,281
Return to the original block. Two borrowed
positions created four subnet

106
00:08:17,281 --> 00:08:21,929
prefixes, and six remaining positions created
sixty-four addresses in each. The

107
00:08:21,929 --> 00:08:26,576
starts, mask, broadcast boundaries, and host
counts all came from that

108
00:08:26,576 --> 00:08:31,224
same division. They are not five unrelated
rules. Subnetting becomes easier

109
00:08:31,224 --> 00:08:35,871
when the arithmetic stays connected to the
picture: fix a longer

110
00:08:35,871 --> 00:08:40,519
beginning, count what remains free, and give
each resulting address range

111
00:08:40,519 --> 00:08:45,167
its correct role.
